WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.480
The Greeks and Romans gave us so much

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.200
that it's little wonder we
continue to sing their praises

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.320
as great inventors,
artists and writers.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.440
But not everything they thought
has stood the test of time.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.840
Here are five of their most absurd
pieces of wisdom.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.200
Number one...

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.880
"If a woman says no she means yes."
- Ovid

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.640
A highly damaging piece of advice

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.520
from the Latin poet Ovid's
Guide to Love.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.120
"Women act coy," he maintained

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.680
"and may even put up a fight,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.440
but secretly they want
to be overcome in battle."

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.840
Now to women today, especially in the
era of #MeToo, it's pretty abhorrent.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.200
In fact, the poem from which
this piece of advice comes

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.560
actually helped
to land Ovid in exile

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.720
for being so sexually explicit.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.160
Two...

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.360
Dark skinned men produce black semen.
- Herodotus

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.840
Herodotus - the so called father of
history - believed this to be true,

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.360
both of Ethiopians
and certain Indians.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.600
To modern eyes it may look racist.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.200
But to him it was little more
than a curiosity worthy of note.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.080
He did not provide a source
for this nugget,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.400
apparently believing it to be true
simply on logical grounds.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.120
Perhaps he thought it was one of
those "Well I never" anecdotes

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.840
that would enliven his histories
of Greece's wars against Persia.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.840
Three...

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:34.680
Aristotle.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.720
Together with Plato,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.720
Aristotle was the most influential
philosopher from antiquity.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.040
He wrote on everything,
from drama to animals.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.920
But he did entertain some rather
peculiar ideas about biology.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.120
He saw men and women
as completely different.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.600
Men were hot and dry.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.240
Women were cold and wet.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.760
If reproduction happens properly,
he thought,

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.560
then the embryo should be male,
like its father.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.400
A daughter is the result
of a man's semen being too weak

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.440
to overpower a woman's
reproductive matter.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.640
Four...

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.880
"Women who see monkeys
during intercourse,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.320
bear children who resemble
monkeys." -Soranus.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.840
A medical marvel from Soranus -
a doctor of the Roman Empire.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.960
His thinking is that you can inherit

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.080
not just the physical characteristics
of your parents,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.360
but even the thoughts they had when
they were actually conceiving you.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.720
Soranus thought it
particularly important

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.400
that a woman be sober during sex

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.720
just in case she conceived
a deranged child.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.720
He also advised her against picturing
monkeys or other animals

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.200
in case the child
came out looking like one.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.640
Far better to picture a statue

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.880
so that the child
might have long graceful limbs,

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.960
but hopefully not be too rigid
and cold.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:56.440
Five...

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.640
"Beware the fifth day of the month,
it is dreadful"

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.600
- Hesiod

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.120
In the 7th Century BC,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.800
a poet called Hesiod composed
a poem on the farmer's year.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.880
He believed that some
days of the month

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.400
were more suitable for some
tasks than others,

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.920
and endeavoured to give land owners
the best possible advice.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.200
Fifth days struck him
as peculiarly threatening.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.280
Hesiod's misgivings
haven't stood the test of time.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.320
But perhaps in the future

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.400
people will look back on us and laugh

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.280
for us finding
Friday the 13th so unlucky.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.780
Thanks for watching. Don't forget to subscribe

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.780
and click the bell to receive notifications for new videos.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.200
See you again soon!

